008538520894944668], [0.0030403053387999535, 1.2425032764440402e-05, 0.00011788542906288058, 3.141540219075978e-06], [5.733836951549165e-05, 0.008509768173098564, 6.043419489287771e-06, 4.706620075012324e-06], [0.008462777361273766, 1.6337004126398824e-05, 1.721907551655022e-06, 5.590215437223378e-07], [4.1362971387570724e-05, 0.00030563329346477985, 1.6038163721532328e-06, 0.002258341759443283], [2.2394572170014726e-06, 0.005199061706662178, 3.2583909614913864e-06, 4.740930762636708e-06]], "new_cot": "1. The body constantly monitors its hydration levels through specialized cells in the brain called osmoreceptors.\n2. When the body detects that it is dehydrated (meaning there is a low water concentration in the blood), the hypothalamus (a region in the brain) is stimulated.\n3. The hypothalamus then signals the posterior pituitary gland to release ADH (also known as vasopressin).\n4. ADH travels through the bloodstream to the kidneys.\n5. Once in the kidneys, ADH acts on the collecting ducts, making them more permeable to water.\n6. This increased permeability allows more water to be reabsorbed from the urine back into the bloodstream, reducing the amount of water lost through urine.\n7. As a result, the body conserves water, and the body's overall water levels begin to increase.\n8. The body also sends signals to the brain, specifically the thirst center, to induce the sensation of thirst.\n9. This encourages the individual to drink more water, further helping to restore proper hydration levels.", "new_cot_probs": [1.2276271263544913e-05, 0.028500253334641457, 2.7392065931053367e-06, 1.1418711665101e-06], "cot_prob": [-1.5463684797286987], "cot_step_prob": [-3.6616358757019043], "mechanistic_diagnostics": {"prompt_lengths": {"answer_prompt_tokens": 278, "removed_answer_prompt_tokens": 254, "step_token_count": 28}, "representation": {"step_answer_cosine_by_layer": [0.1417258083820343, 0.8674344420433044, 0.7544137239456177, 0.7148360013961792, 0.6743907928466797, 0.