{"id": "MCAS_2006_9_43", "question": "A pitcher throws a 0.15 kg baseball at 43 40 m/s towards the catcher. What is the momentum of the baseball while moving at 40 m/s?", "step_idx": 1, "options": ["A): 0.025 kg x m/s", "B): 3.8 kg x m/s", "C): 6.0 kg x m/s", "D): 270 kg x m/s"], "correct": "C", "initial_cot": "1. The formula for momentum (p) is mass (m) times velocity (v): p = m * v.\n2. We are given the mass of the baseball (m) as 0.15 kg and the velocity (v) as 40 m/s.\n3. Plugging in the values, we get p = 0.15 kg * 40 m/s.\n4. Multiplying these values gives us p = 6 kg x m/s.", "initial_cot_probs": [1.6322977103300218e-07, 3.049533461307874e-07, 8.455511124338955e-05, 1.518273506917467e-08], "initial_probs": [5.7376215409021825e-05, 0.00029138047830201685, 0.00017673117690719664, 5.063433854957111e-05], "prediction": 1, "cot_prediction": 2, "adapter_id": "MCAS_2006_9_43_step_1", "global_job_index": 82, "cot_step": "The formula for momentum (p) is mass (m) times velocity (v): p = m * v.\n2.", "segmented_cot": ["1.", "The formula for momentum (p) is mass (m) times velocity (v): p = m * v.\n2.", "We are given the mass of the baseball (m) as 0.15 kg and the velocity (v) as 40 m/s.", "3.", "Plugging in the values, we get p = 0.15 kg * 40 m/s.", "4.", "Multiplying these values gives us p = 6 kg x m/s."], "causal_cot": {"loss": "frodo_reasoning_module", "answer_source": "correct", "counterfactual": "remove_step", "lambda": 0.1, "margin": 1.0, "ie_lambda": 1.0, "margin_lambda": 1.0, "auto_scale": true}, "adapter_record": {"base_model_id": "microsoft/Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct", "adapter_id": "MCAS_2006_9_43_step_1", "adapter_path": "../../../../adapters/arc-challenge/Phi-3/npo_KL_sentencize_s=True_lr=0.0005_rs=1001_pos=True_ff2=False_critical_lora_causal_cot_i09_unfiltered_pos_s0/MCAS_2006_9_43_step_1.pt", "metadata": {"base_model_id": "microsoft/Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct", "dataset": "arc-challenge", "strategy": "sentencize", "stepwise": true, "method": "npo_KL", "instance_i