{"id": "ACTAAP_2014_5_8", "question": "Which best describes how the sun might compare to a different-sized star, if they were right next to each other?", "step_idx": 3, "options": ["A): The sun would be enormous next to an average star.", "B): The sun would be tiny next to one of the largest stars.", "C): The sun would be extremely bright next to an average star.", "D): The sun would be very dim next to one of the smallest stars."], "correct": "B", "initial_cot": "1. The sun is a medium-sized star, known as a G-type main-sequence star (or G dwarf star).\n2. Stars come in a range of sizes, from small red dwarfs to massive blue giants.\n3. If the sun were next to a different-sized star, its size would be compared to that star's size.\n4. Average stars are typically smaller than the sun, so the sun would appear larger next to them.\n5. The largest stars are much larger than the sun, so the sun would appear tiny next to them.\n6. The brightness of a star depends on its size and temperature, not just its size.\n7. The sun is not extremely bright compared to some average stars, which can be brighter due to their size and temperature.\n8. The smallest stars are also not necessarily dimmer than the sun, as some small stars can be very bright due to their high temperatures.", "initial_cot_probs": [1.1402772543078754e-05, 1.8799993995344266e-05, 2.0884907314666634e-07, 1.9425989705723623e-08], "initial_probs": [1.5933173926896416e-05, 0.00028242229018360376, 4.0285372051585e-06, 3.5551718156057177e-06], "prediction": 1, "cot_prediction": 1, "adapter_id": "ACTAAP_2014_5_8_step_3", "global_job_index": 518, "cot_step": "Stars come in a range of sizes, from small red dwarfs to massive blue giants.", "segmented_cot": ["1.", "The sun is a medium-sized star, known as a G-type main-sequence star (or G dwarf star).", "2.", "Stars come in a range of sizes, from small red dwarfs to massive blue giants.", "3.", "If the sun were next to a different-sized star, its size would be compared t