 the independent and dependent variables.\n The independent variable is the one the physicist will change on purpose.\n The dependent variable is the one the physicist will measure to see if it was affected by the independent variable.\n The hypothesis statement is a possible answer of the question based on the physicist's knowledge.\n The control is the constant part of the experiment.\n In this case: The physicist is investigating the effect of the car's speed on the distance it can jump over the ramp. The physicist changes the speed of the car. So, the physicist is changing the independent variable.\n The physicist measures the distance the car jumps over the ramp. This is the dependent variable.\n The hypothesis statement is: \"If the car's speed increases, then the distance it can jump over the ramp will increase.\"\n The control is the ramp.\n So the physicist is investigating the effect of the independent variable on the dependent variable.", "new_cot_probs": [0.00020330022380221635, 1.668789809627924e-05, 3.532828122843057e-05, 0.9991735816001892], "cot_prob": [-0.6833174228668213], "cot_step_prob": [-1.0504345893859863], "mechanistic_diagnostics": {"prompt_lengths": {"answer_prompt_tokens": 217, "removed_answer_prompt_tokens": 197, "step_token_count": 22}, "representation": {"step_answer_cosine_by_layer": [0.053492240607738495, 0.877629816532135, 0.7689309120178223, 0.4253402352333069, 0.4230850636959076, 0.4914255440235138, 0.4970349967479706, 0.5067182779312134, 0.6926115155220032, 0.6707829236984253, 0.6734788417816162, 0.6768906116485596, 0.6607950329780579, 0.6312495470046997, 0.6346898078918457, 0.5779908299446106, 0.5663549900054932, 0.5366048812866211, 0.5621724128723145, 0.6131455302238464, 0.6117867827415466, 0.6213550567626953, 0.6205614805221558, 0.632649302482605, 0.6295642256736755, 0.6627379655838013, 0.6785513162612915, 0.6593908071517944, 0.6956543326377869, 0.7087157964706421, 0.6859073638916016, 0.6772840023040771, 0.7468302845954895], "a