 located in the Cascade Range of the Pacific Northwest region of the United States.\n2.\nThe Cascade Range is part of the Pacific Ring of Fire, which is an area with a lot of volcanic and seismic activity.\n3.\nThe Pacific Ring of Fire is associated with convergent plate boundaries, where two tectonic plates collide.\n4.", "specificity_preds": [1, 2, 0, 2, 0, 2, 0, 2, 2, 0, 0, 1, 2, 0, 3, 0, 1, 0, 3, 1], "specificity_probs": [[5.69548717521684e-07, 0.0007077486370690167, 6.453832384067937e-07, 5.69548717521684e-07], [6.0900138123543e-06, 5.3744183787785005e-06, 0.002456887159496546, 5.3744183787785005e-06], [0.0011659564916044474, 3.2749319416325307e-06, 5.690978355232801e-07, 1.2698288287538162e-07], [9.802103022593656e-07, 0.00011329690460115671, 0.0013802389148622751, 5.640721155941719e-06], [0.0007056420436128974, 1.5435858813361847e-06, 2.3671653082146804e-07, 2.2459039428213146e-06], [3.044031109311618e-05, 2.370693619013764e-05, 0.001466696267016232, 1.7173248352264636e-06], [0.0018905351171270013, 1.2738325494865421e-05, 1.6356332707800902e-05, 4.135528797633015e-06], [5.029599492445413e-07, 3.9170558352452645e-07, 0.0007082203519530594, 5.699282610294176e-07], [3.4228783079015557e-06, 8.827720012050122e-05, 0.0010754363611340523, 3.878629286191426e-06], [0.0018074798863381147, 3.751304393517785e-05, 2.5782317607081495e-05, 5.4581163567490876e-05], [0.0010486706160008907, 0.00014192213711794466, 0.00014192213711794466, 5.221024184720591e-05], [5.74490477447398e-05, 0.0007930596475489438, 0.00048101498396135867, 6.509829836431891e-05], [1.7354183228235343e-06, 1.1316349628032185e-05, 0.0024436512030661106, 2.7146512366016395e-05], [0.003142364788800478, 2.1173085769987665e-05, 2.5287645257776603e-06, 3.6793305753235472e-06], [2.687603455342469e-07, 2.549927330619539e-06, 4.431109061897587e-07, 0.0011656858259811997], [0.0018714733887463808, 7.64828291721642e-06, 4.401283513288945e-05, 1.9337890080350917e-06], [3.7061149669170845e-06, 0.0011644266778603196, 6.440262723117485e-07, 1.0618197165968013e-06], [0.0014953515492379665, 2.547510575823253e-06, 6.441115374400397e-07, 1.4372071177604084e-07], [3.6555186397890793e-06, 2.701083394640591e-05, 9.242598935088608e-07, 0.0008944759028963745], [2.688810241124884e-07, 0.0004290243668947369, 5.692211288987892e-07, 9.38487005441857e-07]], "new_cot": "1. We need to identify the geologic process responsible for creating volcanoes.\n2. Volcanoes are primarily formed by the movement of tectonic plates.\n3. There are three main types of plate boundaries: converging, diverging, and transform faults.\n4. Converging boundaries involve plates moving towards each other, often resulting in subduction zones where one plate slides beneath another, leading to volcanic activity.\n5. Diverging boundaries occur when plates move apart, creating new crust as magma rises to fill the gap, but this typically forms mid-ocean ridges rather than volcanic mountains on continents.\n6. Transform faults involve plates sliding past one another horizontally, which doesn't typically create volcanoes.\n7. Rift zones are areas where the Earth's crust is being pulled apart, similar to diverging boundaries, but they also can be associated with volcanic activity due to the thinning crust and rising magma.\n8. Mount St. Helens is a stratovolcano, which is a type of volcano built up by many layers (strata) of hardened lava, tephra, pumice, and volcanic ash.\n9. Stratovolcanoes are commonly formed at converging plate boundaries where subduction occurs.", "new_cot_probs": [0.0007088501588441432, 1.4422859351270745e-07, 9.912676546264265e-08, 1.341534794363497e-08], "cot_prob": [-1.3167731761932373], "cot_step_prob": [-5.040846347808838], "mechanistic_diagnostics": {"prompt_lengths": {"answer_prompt_tokens": 301, "removed_answer_prompt_tokens": 275, "step_token_count": 31}, "representation": {"step_answer_cosine_by_layer": [0.02023771032691002, 0.8661140203475952, 0.776729941368103, 0.7490493655204773, 0.7056976556777