{"id": "TIMSS_1995_8_J1", "question": "Which BEST describes the surface of the Earth over billions of years?", "step_idx": 7, "options": ["A): A flat surface is gradually pushed up into higher and higher mountains until the Earth is covered with mountains.", "B): High mountains gradually wear down until most of the Earth is at sea level.", "C): High mountains gradually wear down as new mountains are continuously being formed, over and over again.", "D): High mountains and flat plains stay side by side for billions of years with little change."], "correct": "C", "initial_cot": "1. The Earth's surface is dynamic and constantly changing due to various geological processes.\n2. Mountains are formed by tectonic forces, volcanic activity, and erosion.\n3. Over time, mountains are subjected to weathering and erosion, which gradually wear them down.\n4. New mountains can form through tectonic activity, but this process is not continuous and does not happen at the same rate as erosion.\n5. The Earth's surface is not flat, and mountains do not remain at the same height for billions of years.", "initial_cot_probs": [5.2480373824437265e-08, 1.8684308088268153e-05, 4.4821321353083476e-05, 1.170995478361192e-08], "initial_probs": [1.314073756475409e-06, 3.389041012269445e-05, 0.0007713421364314854, 1.911966137413401e-06], "prediction": 2, "cot_prediction": 2, "adapter_id": "TIMSS_1995_8_J1_step_7", "global_job_index": 648, "cot_step": "New mountains can form through tectonic activity, but this process is not continuous and does not happen at the same rate as erosion.", "segmented_cot": ["1.", "The Earth's surface is dynamic and constantly changing due to various geological processes.", "2.", "Mountains are formed by tectonic forces, volcanic activity, and erosion.", "3.", "Over time, mountains are subjected to weathering and erosion, which gradually wear them down.", "4.", "New mountains can form through tectonic activity, but this process is not continuous and does not happen at the same rate as erosion.", "5.", "The Earth's surface is not flat, and mountains do not remain at the same height for billions of years."], "causal_cot": {"loss": "frodo_reasoning_module", "answer_source": "correct", "counterfactual": "remove_step", "lambda": 0.1, "margin": 1.0, "ie_lambda": 1.0, "margin_lambda": 1.0, "auto_scale": true}, "adapter_record": {"base_model_id": "microsoft/Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct", "adapter_id": "TIMSS_1995_8_J1_step_7", "adapter_path": "../../../../adapters/arc-challenge/Phi-3/npo_KL_sentencize_s=True_lr=0.0005_rs=1001_pos=True_ff2=False_critical_lora_causal_cot_i09_unfiltered_pos_s0/TIMSS_1995_8_J1_step_7.pt", "metadata": {"base_model_id": "microsoft/Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct", "dataset": "arc-challenge", "strategy": "sentencize", "stepwise": true, "method": "npo_KL", "instance_id": "TIMSS_1995_8_J1", "step_idx": 7, "target_index": 81, "global_job_index": 648, "question": "Which BEST describes the surface of the Earth over billions of years?", "causal_cot": {"loss": "frodo_reasoning_module", "answer_source": "correct", "counterfactual": "remove_step", "lambda": 0.1, "margin": 1.0, "ie_lambda": 1.0, "margin_lambda": 1.0, "auto_scale": true}}}, "adapter_record_path": "v2_outputs/adapter_records/arc-challenge/Phi-3/npo_KL_sentencize_s=True_lr=0.0005_rs=1001_pos=True_ff2=False_critical_lora_causal_cot_i09_unfiltered_pos_s0/TIMSS_1995_8_J1_step_7.json", "adapter_training_history": [{"epoch": 1, "mean_loss": 15.249238014221191, "evaluation": null}, {"epoch": 2, "mean_loss": 2.4478673934936523, "evaluation": null}, {"epoch": 3, "mean_loss": 0.06465447694063187, "evaluation": null}, {"epoch": 4, "mean_loss": 0.093016617000103, "evaluation": null}, {"epoch": 5, "mean_loss": 0.25399649143218994, "evaluation": {"completion": "B).\n\nThe reasoning is as follows:", "probs": [7.652875666508407e-09, 1.2210865861561615e-05, 1.4583800975742633e-06, 1.8358294440190548e-08], "prediction": 1, "target_cot_step": "New mountains can form through tectonic activ