 be tiny next to one of the largest stars.\n(C): The sun would be extremely bright next to an average star.\n(D): The sun would be very dim next to one of the smallest stars.\n\nAssistant: Let's think step by step:\n\n1.\nThe sun is a medium-sized star, known as a G-type main-sequence star (or G dwarf star).\n2.\nStars come in a range of sizes, from small red dwarfs to massive blue giants.\n3.\nIf the sun were next to a different-sized star, its size would be compared to that star's size.\n4.\nAverage stars are typically smaller than the sun, so the sun would appear larger next to them.\n5.\nThe largest stars are much larger than the sun, so the sun would appear tiny next to them.\n6.", "specificity_preds": [1, 2, 0, 2, 0, 2, 0, 2, 2, 0, 0, 1, 2, 0, 3, 0, 1, 0, 3, 1], "specificity_probs": [[7.721182271325233e-08, 0.00015819007239770144, 7.721182271325233e-08, 1.1234254060354942e-07], [8.265286055575416e-07, 1.5441588629983016e-06, 0.0005497586098499596, 1.2025921023450792e-06], [9.573354327585548e-05, 5.023634912504349e-07, 4.672708797670566e-08, 1.1814456968295417e-08], [4.3932800508628134e-07, 4.4812677515437827e-05, 0.00022757751867175102, 3.2462191938975593e-06], [0.00026015087496489286, 5.022093887419032e-07, 9.889088659065237e-08, 4.4319816083771e-07], [8.639824045530986e-06, 6.728701464453479e-06, 0.00025249263853766024, 7.091999236763513e-07], [0.00032642806763760746, 3.2001880754251033e-06, 5.978738499834435e-06, 1.1772833659051685e-06], [1.8505031107451941e-07, 2.6924658413918223e-07, 0.00033457876997999847, 3.917514561635471e-07], [1.2010212913082796e-06, 5.1068724133074284e-05, 0.00037734967190772295, 1.5421419448102824e-06], [0.0006660129874944687, 1.3822658729623072e-05, 9.500165106146596e-06, 2.011185097217094e-05], [0.00017572047363501042, 8.300447370857e-05, 7.325119076995179e-05, 3.920853851013817e-05], [5.437040454125963e-05, 0.00040174595778808, 0.00021503913740161806, 4.798172085429542e-05], [4.934199751005508e-07, 3.6459080092754448e-06, 0.0006