00012309473822824657, 3.917835726952035e-07, 6.80817748843765e-08, 6.80817748843765e-08], "prediction": 0, "target_cot_step": "Carbon dioxide (CO2) dissolves in water to form carbonic acid (H2CO3), but it does not produce ions that can conduct electricity.", "target_cot_step_prefix": "Human: Question: Which substance, when mixed with water, will produce the best conductor of electricity?\n\nChoices:\n(A): table salt\n(B): granulated sugar\n(C): carbon dioxide\n(D): motor oil\n\nAssistant: Let's think step by step:\n\n1.\nWe need to find a substance that, when mixed with water, will produce a good conductor of electricity.\n2.\nA good conductor of electricity is a material that allows electric current to flow through it easily.\n3.\nIn this case, we are looking for a substance that can dissolve in water and form ions, as ions are responsible for conducting electricity in a solution.\n4.\nAmong the given options, table salt (sodium chloride) is the only substance that dissolves in water and forms ions (Na+ and Cl-).\n5.\nWhen table salt dissolves in water, it dissociates into sodium ions (Na+) and chloride ions (Cl-), which can carry electric current through the solution.\n6.\nGranulated sugar (sucrose) does not dissociate into ions in water, so it will not conduct electricity.\n7.", "specificity_preds": [1, 2, 0, 2, 0, 2, 0, 2, 2, 0, 0, 1, 2, 0, 3, 0, 1, 0, 3, 1], "specificity_probs": [[7.721182271325233e-08, 0.00015819007239770144, 7.721182271325233e-08, 1.1234254060354942e-07], [8.265286055575416e-07, 1.5441588629983016e-06, 0.0005497586098499596, 1.2025921023450792e-06], [9.573354327585548e-05, 5.023634912504349e-07, 4.672708797670566e-08, 1.1814456968295417e-08], [4.3932800508628134e-07, 4.4812677515437827e-05, 0.00022757751867175102, 3.2462191938975593e-06], [0.00026015087496489286, 5.022093887419032e-07, 9.889088659065237e-08, 4.4319816083771e-07], [8.639824045530986e-06, 6.728701464453479e-06, 0.00025249263853766024, 7.091999236763513e-07], [0.0003264280676376