, 0.2890625, 0.375, 0.392578125, 0.3828125]}, "projections": [], "warnings": []}}}], "unlearning_results": {"0": {"completion": "A) table salt.\n\nReasoning", "probs": [0.00012309473822824657, 3.917835726952035e-07, 6.80817748843765e-08, 6.80817748843765e-08], "prediction": 0, "target_cot_step": "Motor oil is a non-polar substance and does not dissolve in water, so it will not produce a conductive solution.", "target_cot_step_prefix": "Human: Question: Which substance, when mixed with water, will produce the best conductor of electricity?\n\nChoices:\n(A): table salt\n(B): granulated sugar\n(C): carbon dioxide\n(D): motor oil\n\nAssistant: Let's think step by step:\n\n1.\nWe need to find a substance that, when mixed with water, will produce a good conductor of electricity.\n2.\nA good conductor of electricity is a material that allows electric current to flow through it easily.\n3.\nIn this case, we are looking for a substance that can dissolve in water and form ions, as ions are responsible for conducting electricity in a solution.\n4.\nAmong the given options, table salt (sodium chloride) is the only substance that dissolves in water and forms ions (Na+ and Cl-).\n5.\nWhen table salt dissolves in water, it dissociates into sodium ions (Na+) and chloride ions (Cl-), which can carry electric current through the solution.\n6.\nGranulated sugar (sucrose) does not dissociate into ions in water, so it will not conduct electricity.\n7.\nCarbon dioxide (CO2) dissolves in water to form carbonic acid (H2CO3), but it does not produce ions that can conduct electricity.\n8.", "specificity_preds": [1, 2, 0, 2, 0, 2, 0, 2, 2, 0, 0, 1, 2, 0, 3, 0, 1, 0, 3, 1], "specificity_probs": [[7.721182271325233e-08, 0.00015819007239770144, 7.721182271325233e-08, 1.1234254060354942e-07], [8.265286055575416e-07, 1.5441588629983016e-06, 0.0005497586098499596, 1.2025921023450792e-06], [9.573354327585548e-05, 5.023634912504349e-07, 4.672708797670566e-08, 1.1814456968295417e-08], [4.3932800508628